CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional coordinate plot showing two vectors in standard position together with both of their cross products. The axes are thin black lines carrying evenly spaced tick marks: a vertical one labeled z, one descending to the lower right labeled y, and one descending to the lower left labeled x. Two short orange arrows leave the origin, one pointing to the right and labeled u = <0, 2, 1>, the other pointing down and to the left and labeled v = <3, -1, 0>. A long red arrow runs from the origin downward and to the right, reaching the bottom of the frame, labeled u x v = <1, 3, -6>. A long teal arrow runs from the origin upward and slightly to the left, reaching the top of the frame, labeled v x u = <-1, -3, 6>. Each long arrow is wrapped in a dashed blue rectangular box whose edges project its tip back onto the coordinate directions, making the endpoint coordinates readable. Drawing both products from a common origin makes it plain that they lie along a single line but point in opposite senses, each standing perpendicular to the plane spanned by u and v. The plot supports the relation u x v = -(v x u).

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - x-axis: thin black line running from upper-right to lower-left, arrowhead at lower-left end, labeled bold italic 'x', evenly spaced tick marks (unit ticks, ~±5 units)
  - y-axis: thin black line running from upper-left to lower-right, arrowhead at lower-right end, labeled bold italic 'y', evenly spaced unit tick marks (~±5 units)
  - z-axis: vertical black line with arrowhead at top, labeled bold italic 'z', unit tick marks (~±7 units)
  - origin point where all three axes meet (center of frame)
  - orange arrow u from origin to (0, 2, 1), short, pointing right and slightly up on screen, label 'u = ⟨0, 2, 1⟩' to the right of its tip
  - orange arrow v from origin to (3, -1, 0), short, pointing down-left on screen, label 'v = ⟨3, -1, 0⟩' below-left of its tip
  - red arrow u × v from origin to (1, 3, -6), long, running down and to the right to the bottom of the frame, label 'u × v = ⟨1, 3, -6⟩' at the bottom
  - teal arrow v × u from origin to (-1, -3, 6), long, running up and slightly to the left to the top of the frame, label 'v × u = ⟨-1, -3, 6⟩' at the top
  - dashed blue rectangular box (projection box) from origin to the tip (1, 3, -6), 12 dashed edges, showing the x, y, z components of u × v
  - dashed blue rectangular box from origin to the tip (-1, -3, 6), 12 dashed edges, showing the components of v × u
  - optional translucent parallelogram/plane spanned by u and v (shown only during the right-hand-rule walkthrough)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Right-handed coordinate frame, 1 world unit = 1 axis unit. Axes: x from -5 to +5 (screen: +x toward lower-left with arrowhead, standard isometric), y from -5 to +5 (+y toward lower-right with arrowhead), z from -7 to +7 (+z straight up, arrowhead on top). Unit tick marks every 1 unit as short perpendicular dashes, ~0.18 units long. Vectors from the origin: u = (0, 2, 1) and v = (3, -1, 0) drawn as short orange shafts with cone arrowheads (#E8703A / orange-vermilion); u × v = (1, 3, -6) in red (#D94A3D), v × u = (-1, -3, 6) in teal (#12807C). Verify: u × v = (2·0 - 1·(-1), 1·3 - 0·0, 0·(-1) - 2·3) = (1, 3, -6). Each long arrow is enclosed by a dashed axis-aligned rectangular box with opposite corners at the origin and the arrow tip — so the u × v box spans x∈[0,1], y∈[0,3], z∈[-6,0] and the v × u box spans x∈[-1,0], y∈[-3,0], z∈[0,6]; dashed steel-blue lines (#2B5F87), dash ~0.2 / gap ~0.15. Axes and ticks thin black (#000). Labels in black, bold italic single letters for axes; component labels placed just beyond each arrow tip: 'u = ⟨0, 2, 1⟩' right of u's tip, 'v = ⟨3, -1, 0⟩' below-left of v's tip, 'u × v = ⟨1, 3, -6⟩' at the bottom of the frame, 'v × u = ⟨-1, -3, 6⟩' at the top. Optional u–v parallelogram (vertices 0, u, u+v, v) as a 15%-opacity gray fill shown only in right-hand-rule steps 2-3. Overall scene bounding half-size ≈ 7 units vertically; frame the z extent ±7 to fit both long arrows.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
